WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.401 --> 00:00:07.920
SIERRA: Okay, so the funny thing about recording&nbsp;
a podcast from your home during a quarantine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:13.560
is that you might end up spending a lot of&nbsp;
time in your closet. It may seem strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.040
but you get the best sound in a&nbsp;
small room with lots of padding.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:23.460
So, the reason I’m telling you this, is because&nbsp;
today’s episode is about clothes. And there’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:29.220
better place to talk about them than here,&nbsp;
in my very full, very disorganized closet.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:35.940
The thing is, I love shopping. Shopping&nbsp;
makes me feel good. It’s exciting.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.560
And the decision about whether or not&nbsp;
to buy something has always boiled down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.440
to two things. One, how badly I wanted it,&nbsp;
and two, whether or not I could afford it.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.660
But what if the real cost of my clothes&nbsp;
has nothing to do with the price tag?

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.560
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It Matters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.840
today what is Fast Fashion? And&nbsp;
what is it doing to our world?

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.760
Mean Girls:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.280
Get in loser we’re going shopping.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.020
Sex and the City: I spent 40,000&nbsp;
on shoes and have nowhere to live?

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.053
Queer Eye: Yas! Yas!

00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:15.780
Emily Mariko:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:21.166
There are a ton of matching separates out&nbsp;
there in stores right now and it's really fun.

00:01:21.166 --> 00:01:23.220
Shea Whitney: Their trends&nbsp;
are just always on point.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:30.708
INMYSEAMS: I grabbed I think 17 items&nbsp;
...the top was $14.99, the skirt was $34.99.

00:01:30.708 --> 00:01:35.867
Teen Vogue: We all love to shop and these days the&nbsp;
newest styles and clothes are cheaper than ever.

00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:39.180
John Oliver: We buy a lot&nbsp;
of clothes in this country.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.680
SIERRA: So what is fast fashion?

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.020
GREER: So what fast fashion means is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.720
just the rate of turnover of clothing&nbsp;
in stores every several weeks.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.280
I'm Linda Greer and I'm an environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.860
toxicologist. I study and advocate on&nbsp;
industrial toxic chemical pollution.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:12.420
So, back in the day, stores basically had&nbsp;
four seasons and you went in seasonally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.920
to buy whatever clothing that you&nbsp;
wanted. And your expectations were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.940
not such that if you went back in two&nbsp;
weeks, you would buy it all over again.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:21.420
SIERRA: Sure.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:27.180
GREER: But then, with fast fashion, basically,&nbsp;
this dramatic lowering of prices and a new sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.340
of marketing model that draws consumers&nbsp;
in, at any time to see what's new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:39.060
what we found was this immense acceleration of&nbsp;
people buying clothing and people buying things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:45.120
that they might even never wear. Or only wear&nbsp;
a few times, and it's sort of a whole mindset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:51.900
shift from buying things we really love and&nbsp;
buying things you think will last, sort of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.300
timeless fashion, to use a really dated term.&nbsp;
Which would probably be my parents' generation.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.306
SIERRA: Right. As opposed to me when I'm like,&nbsp;
"I'm going out tonight. I should get a shirt."

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:02.160
GREER: I need something new.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:02.520
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.920
GREER: Right. Not to mention,&nbsp;
you know, I think the impact of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.800
Instagram where people feel like they&nbsp;
need to look different on every shot.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:08.220
SIERRA: Sure.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.760
GREER: All of these things have had&nbsp;
a very corrosive effect because, from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.160
an environmental standpoint, that's just massive&nbsp;
throughput. That's just massive amounts of fabric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.740
massive amounts of apparel, you know, coming&nbsp;
into these stores and it's fair to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:27.720
almost no matter what these factories&nbsp;
do if they're making that much stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:33.920
their carbon footprint, water use, and chemical&nbsp;
use, and pollution is just very hard to curtail.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.940
SEGRAN: When we think about how&nbsp;
enormous the fashion industry is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:46.320
it starts to become clear what kind&nbsp;
of impact it has. Hi. I'm Liz Segran.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.320
I'm the senior staff writer at Fast Company&nbsp;
magazine and I focus on the fashion industry.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:04:00.120
So for instance, in 2015, a hundred&nbsp;
billion articles of clothing were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.320
manufactured. And if you think about&nbsp;
how there's just over seven billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.740
people on the planet that is&nbsp;
a lot of clothing per person.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.520
SIERRA: A hundred billion&nbsp;
pieces of clothing a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.940
and I’m probably buying more than my fair share.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.280
And what have I gotten out of them? Looking&nbsp;
around my closet there’s a lot of things I love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.920
and still wear. But there are also some&nbsp;
I’ve never worn, and quite a few I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:32.280
only worn once or twice. They served their brief&nbsp;
purpose, and are now just kinda taking up space.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:39.360
SEGRAN: Given how the fast fashion industry&nbsp;
works, the clothes that are on the shelves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:44.280
are really sort of designed to be on trend for the&nbsp;
season. So that article of clothing that you buy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.240
you know, you're probably going to&nbsp;
wear that for a season, two seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.220
at most a couple of years and then you're going&nbsp;
to chuck that away. And so basically all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.100
carbon that was involved in shipping&nbsp;
all of those items across the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:01.020
all of the labor that went into that,&nbsp;
all of the chemicals that went into that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:05.940
it basically goes into a very short-term purchase&nbsp;
ultimately, and if you just think about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.620
magnified by every human on the planet, you begin&nbsp;
to get a sense of how enormous this industry is.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:15.600
SIERRA: According to the United Nations&nbsp;
Alliance for Sustainable Fashion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:22.380
the $2.4 trillion apparel industry accounts&nbsp;
for 8 to 10% of the world’s greenhouse gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:29.220
emissions. That’s more than all international&nbsp;
flights and maritime shipping combined. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:33.240
are staggering numbers. But it’s important&nbsp;
to take them with a grain of salt.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:40.320
SIERRA: So this obviously sounds like a huge&nbsp;
issue. How reliable are the statistics in this?

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.620
GREER: Ah. So this is the point&nbsp;
in which the podcast needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.820
be a video so I can be wringing my&nbsp;
hands so that people could see. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:52.140
numbers that are associated with this&nbsp;
industry are extremely poor quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:57.920
This has made me crazy because they've&nbsp;
been poor quality for almost a decade.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:04.560
SEGRAN: I think that the fashion industry is&nbsp;
a very complex global industry, but there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.660
other highly complex global industries out there&nbsp;
like the transportation industry, like the oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:16.320
industry, and yet we have relatively good figures&nbsp;
about the impact that they have. And I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.820
that's because these industries have been under&nbsp;
scrutiny for longer and there are government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:27.120
imposed consequences on them. So as a result,&nbsp;
there's been more academic research, government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.960
research done to assess these industries, and&nbsp;
this has not happened in the fashion industry.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:38.580
SIERRA: Part of the problem is that production&nbsp;
in the fashion industry is piecemeal. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.640
it’s likely that the materials in the&nbsp;
shirt you’re wearing passed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:45.420
middleman after middleman, across&nbsp;
many countries, before you bought it.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:50.640
The path varies, but let’s take a hypothetical&nbsp;
cotton shirt. So, the cotton might be grown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:57.900
in Brazil, the U.S., or maybe even Uzbekistan.&nbsp;
Once harvested it’s off to, say, China or India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:04.380
to be twisted into yarn, or woven into fabric.&nbsp;
Then it gets dyed. But it’s not over yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:10.560
the fabric is off to another location, maybe&nbsp;
Bangladesh, Vietnam, or Honduras to be cut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.480
trimmed, and assembled — this is all before
it can be shipped to a store or your doorstep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.540
as a finished product.
All along the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:24.980
those materials are leaving a trail of wastewater,&nbsp;
industrial chemicals, air pollution, and carbon.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:31.200
SEGRAN: There are so many people involved&nbsp;
with the fashion industry. And so basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:37.800
companies are only working with a couple of&nbsp;
players in their supply chain at any one time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:45.480
And so if you ask a company to survey itself and&nbsp;
provide data about the impact that it's having,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:49.800
it's only going to actually know a couple&nbsp;
of the most recent steps in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:56.700
right. And so I think it's just a very, very&nbsp;
difficult thing to do to sort of trace a garment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:01.320
all the way back to the raw materials that&nbsp;
it was made of. I previously mentioned that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:06.540
a hundred billion articles of clothing&nbsp;
were made in 2015. 15 years before that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:12.900
in 2000 only 50 billion articles of clothing&nbsp;
were manufactured. So in that 15 year period, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:18.540
number of clothes manufactured globally doubled.&nbsp;
And actually according to surveys, there are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:22.860
garments that some of us buy that are only worn&nbsp;
eight to ten times before we throw them away.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:26.820
SIERRA: I mean, what's wild about this&nbsp;
is that this seems to have gotten worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.100
since the world has sort of&nbsp;
woken up to climate change.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.020
SEGRAN: We're seeing all of these horrible things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:35.340
happening and we're seeing exactly how&nbsp;
bad the climate crisis is going to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:40.220
and yet this is still the infrastructure&nbsp;
through which we buy our clothes.

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:45.300
SIERRA: It’s hard to learn that something&nbsp;
you love is harming the world around you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.320
And that made me want to talk to someone&nbsp;
who loves fashion as much as I do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:55.640
someone in the industry. So I did what anyone&nbsp;
in my situation would do, I called a supermodel.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:03.780
VALLETTA: My name is Amber Valletta and I'm&nbsp;
a model, an activist, and an entrepreneur.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:07.800
SIERRA: Aside from being seen on the&nbsp;
runway and on the cover of Vogue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.420
Amber formed her own company called Master and&nbsp;
Muse. And she had some really great advice.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:20.460
VALLETTA: I started thinking about what I wanted&nbsp;
to do with my, my life, my career. And sort of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:27.420
you know, what kind of legacy did I wanna leave&nbsp;
behind for my son. So that was the impetus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:33.300
for me to start Master and Muse, which was an&nbsp;
online store for responsibly made fashion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:40.260
accessories, and sort of the thought leadership&nbsp;
and work that I've been doing in fashion to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:47.160
get the fashion industry to become aware of&nbsp;
sustainability, but also consumers and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:53.640
we can think differently about our clothes. So I&nbsp;
suggest, you know, in the next few days, you go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:59.700
into your closet and your dresser drawers and you&nbsp;
look at what you have and you really take stock.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:05.040
SIERRA: Ok so Amber told me to take&nbsp;
stock, so let’s take stock. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:09.780
so there's a lot of stuff in here. There is a&nbsp;
little bit of a method to this madness. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:16.980
shoes down there and there's long sleeve shirts&nbsp;
up here. And then to the right we have pants. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:23.460
shirt I have in three versions. Jump cut to, I’ve&nbsp;
worn the white one and not the other two at all.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:29.580
So looking around at all this stuff I am&nbsp;
realizing that a lot of it is, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:35.760
fast fashion, and a lot of it doesn’t get&nbsp;
used, and that never bothered me before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:42.000
because it was cheap, or at least affordable,&nbsp;
and yeah it’s starting to bother me now.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:49.560
SIERRA: So, okay, I know the clothes we&nbsp;
wear travel all across the world while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:54.060
they’re being made, but could&nbsp;
you walk me through, sort of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:59.220
the environmental impact at each of those&nbsp;
stages? How is my shirt polluting the world?

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:02.820
GREER: Yeah. So would you like&nbsp;
to talk about a cotton shirt?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:11.760
So, first of all, cotton is a very difficult&nbsp;
crop to grow. It is a very thirsty crop. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.740
means it needs a lot of water. It is hammered&nbsp;
by insects, so it requires a lot of pesticides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:22.140
And in order to get a good yield, it really&nbsp;
likes to have fertilizer. And so when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:27.900
look at the cultivation of cotton, it is not&nbsp;
a walk in the park. In fact, that crop alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:33.480
uses more than 15% of all the insecticides&nbsp;
we use for any crop anywhere in the world.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:34.020
SIERRA: Wow.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:40.320
GREER: It then needs to be, sort of processed&nbsp;
into fibers, and prior to doing the spinning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:48.240
it needs to be scoured and sort of hammered away&nbsp;
with a lot of detergent and scouring agents to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:54.120
take off the natural waxes that come along with&nbsp;
the cotton ball. So, you know, it goes through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:59.640
ginner and then it gets really hammered away and&nbsp;
then it finally gets spun. In order to get woven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:05.700
it needs to be coated with a chemical because the&nbsp;
fibers themselves are not very strong. They're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:11.100
strong enough to be woven without sizing on them.&nbsp;
Sizing is sort of like a starch that gets put on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:17.400
that later when it gets washed off, is very bad&nbsp;
for the rivers that receive it. And then when it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:23.700
finally woven that starch has to be taken off,&nbsp;
and we're finally ready to dye it and finish it.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:25.413
SIERRA: Wow. That's already a lot&nbsp;
of chemicals you just mentioned.

00:12:25.413 --> 00:12:28.920
GREER: It's already a lot, and I gotta&nbsp;
tell ya, one more thing about cotton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:35.160
is that it doesn't like to accept dye. And&nbsp;
so they need to add more chemicals to allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:40.920
those things to come together, including&nbsp;
salt. So, overall for a fiber that gets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:45.720
I think for many people, sort of a&nbsp;
free ride, it is a problematic fiber.

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:50.100
SIERRA: The UN estimates that a&nbsp;
single pair of jeans requires a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.240
kilogram of cotton. And because cotton&nbsp;
tends to be grown in dry environments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.240 --> 00:13:00.180
producing enough for that pair of jeans consumes&nbsp;
about 10 years of drinking water for one person.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:07.200
When scaled up, this can be devastating. In&nbsp;
2014, NASA revealed images showing that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:12.840
Aral Sea had effectively disappeared after rivers&nbsp;
were diverted for cotton farms in Uzbekistan.

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:15.780
And fabric production doesn’t just consume water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:21.120
it pollutes it too. Roughly 8,000&nbsp;
synthetic chemicals are used across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:27.000
the world to produce textiles. Accounting&nbsp;
for 17 - 20% of industrial water pollution.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.800
SIERRA: So, it really seems like, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:35.940
this shirt leaves a chemical and carbon&nbsp;
trail all the way across the world.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:41.400
GREER: Yes. So I would like you to think about&nbsp;
your clothing as having embedded carbon in them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:47.460
Think about, it looks just like a shirt, but&nbsp;
how much energy went into that shirt that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:52.740
complicit with that carbon emission even if it&nbsp;
didn't happen in your country. And then similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:59.580
it has embedded water use and it has embedded&nbsp;
chemical use and we don't see those embedded, sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:05.400
of footprints, in any of the things that we buy.&nbsp;
And, by the way, it's very difficult to get that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:10.800
information. So I certainly don't blame consumers&nbsp;
for not having a little quantitative calculator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:16.800
in their head of where their biggest embedded&nbsp;
carbon lies. But when they buy clothing, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:22.980
a lot of environmental impact that was left in&nbsp;
the wake of the manufacturing of that stuff.

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:27.780
SEGRAN: I actually think that it's the&nbsp;
accumulation of all of those steps that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:33.840
the problem, and I think that that's partly why&nbsp;
it's hard for the average person to get a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:40.080
of how enormous the problem is and sometimes&nbsp;
we see news stories about things like, "oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:44.940
you know, what is the impact of e-commerce?”&nbsp;
Right, like “how much energy is used to ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:50.820
those products to us?” That is one tiny part&nbsp;
of this whole process, the only part that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:56.520
are able to visualize or make sense of. And I&nbsp;
actually think that part of the reason that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:03.240
fashion industry has gotten away with so much&nbsp;
environmental destruction is because the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:09.300
supply chain is so broken up and there's so many&nbsp;
middlemen along the way that it's really hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:15.000
to point a finger at any one part of this chain&nbsp;
and make somebody take responsibility for that.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.040
SIERRA: What sort of regulation&nbsp;
is happening currently?

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:19.380
SEGRAN: There's hardly any at all.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:26.460
GREER: This is a really low-tech industry, it&nbsp;
is operating much the way it operated, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:31.320
decades ago. I think that the difference is&nbsp;
that it's operating in the developing world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:36.600
in economies like China, in transition.&nbsp;
So, when it was operating here in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:42.840
United States and Europe, which was really only&nbsp;
about 15 or even 20 years ago it was operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:48.000
under a very mature environmental regulatory&nbsp;
system that was in place by our government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:53.820
the Environmental Protection Agency, that&nbsp;
had rules, regulations, it had inspections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:59.220
and enforcement and the industry was very&nbsp;
well controlled. What happened then was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:03.600
you know, the big wave of globalization&nbsp;
where our manufacturing left our shores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:08.640
sort of in search for cheaper, cheaper,&nbsp;
goods, which now we all enjoy purchasing.

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:12.300
SIERRA: That's so interesting. You don't&nbsp;
think about it moving backwards that way.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:16.200
GREER: No. And I have to say, you know,&nbsp;
I worked for the Natural Resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:20.820
Defense Council for almost 30 years. We&nbsp;
observed the pattern of globalization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:27.600
of course. We watched the trade agreements, et&nbsp;
cetera, get negotiated and we did push back on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:32.640
some of the weaknesses in terms of environmental&nbsp;
matters in those agreements. But I have to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:39.900
we did underestimate the environmental impact&nbsp;
of globalization. We really didn't think that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:45.000
you know, more than 80% of our, let's say,&nbsp;
textile factories would leave this country.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:51.660
SEGRAN: I think a lot of brands are at this point&nbsp;
trying to self-regulate. You see lots of brands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:57.780
making promises about how they're, you know,&nbsp;
trying to be carbon neutral or carbon positive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.780 --> 00:17:02.700
and there are other brands that are saying,&nbsp;
"Oh, we're not going to use anymore virgin or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.060
new plastic in our clothing, we're&nbsp;
going to use recycled plastic.”

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:09.780
GREER: A lot of these companies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:13.020
a small effort underway. Some&nbsp;
of them are just greenwashing.

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:14.520
SIERRA: And what's greenwashing?

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:17.820
GREER: Greenwashing is when they pretend&nbsp;
that they care, but they don't care.

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:23.460
VALLETTA: You know, these companies that get on&nbsp;
the bandwagon about, you know, a new packaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:29.700
that might be eco-friendly, or maybe they claim to&nbsp;
be organic but they're only partially organic, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:34.100
sometimes you find out that&nbsp;
they're not doing a whole lot.

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:39.240
SIERRA: Linda and Amber are right,&nbsp;
phony sustainability won’t actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:43.020
help the environment in the long&nbsp;
run. But some people think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.900
greenwashing might actually be&nbsp;
a good sign for the short term.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:51.540
SEGRAN: My take on this is that I actually&nbsp;
think that it's really great that brands are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:57.540
now feeling pressure to make these claims&nbsp;
about sustainability. I think that is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:03.300
important first step because for a long&nbsp;
time brands were manufacturing with highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:09.540
polluting practices with impunity. Now, I see&nbsp;
this as a sign that these brands are responding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:16.320
to consumers’ desire for more eco-friendly&nbsp;
manufacturing and more eco-friendly clothing.

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:20.700
SIERRA: Right. I mean, the idea that it's a&nbsp;
marketing tool at all seems to be a good sign.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:25.560
SEGRAN: I think so, yeah, and so now the next&nbsp;
step is just holding these brands into account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:30.360
and kind of putting pressure on them to raise&nbsp;
the bar and raise their standards of what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:36.480
really means to be eco-friendly. What we need,&nbsp;
is we need governments to force companies to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:40.920
responsibility for the pollution that they're&nbsp;
producing and the impact that they're having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:45.620
because right now, you know, it's citizens who&nbsp;
are paying the price for all of this destruction.

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:49.560
SIERRA: There is reason to&nbsp;
be hopeful. Some countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:53.640
like France, have started making moves in the&nbsp;
right direction, and addressing the problem.

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:59.460
SEGRAN: So in France, there's a woman. She&nbsp;
is part of the Ministry of Ecological and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:04.620
Inclusive Transition, which is basically France's&nbsp;
department in the government that's devoted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:11.700
sustainability, and her name is Brune Poirson.&nbsp;
And she has made the fashion industry kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:17.520
her pet project. And so some of the things that&nbsp;
she's done for instance is she has banned fashion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:23.160
companies from destroying their own merchandise.&nbsp;
Now, that should seem like an obvious thing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:29.220
a brand not to be doing, but it's actually pretty&nbsp;
common in the fashion industry particularly among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:33.780
high fashion brands to basically destroy&nbsp;
their merchandise at the end of a season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:37.920
anything that's unsold because they&nbsp;
don't want those products to end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:43.140
on a second-hand market or to be discounted&nbsp;
because that will impact the brand equity.

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:45.660
SIERRA: Wow. That's awful by the way.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:51.060
SEGRAN: It's so terrible. So these beautiful&nbsp;
luxury handbags have been essentially incinerated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:55.980
and burnt for a long time. But actually this&nbsp;
is also happening in the fast fashion sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:59.280
because in the fast fashion sector they&nbsp;
have a completely different problem which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:04.320
is that they tend to produce so many clothes&nbsp;
every season that they just have this huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:08.880
stockpile of things that they just can't get&nbsp;
rid of. There's no way for them to sell it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:13.200
or donate it all. So they're also burning&nbsp;
it. And so if you think about all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:16.800
steps that went into producing something&nbsp;
that will just ultimately get incinerated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:23.280
it's just horrible to think about. So in France,&nbsp;
they have banned this practice so companies cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:27.600
do this anymore. And that actually has a&nbsp;
ripple effect, where these companies then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:31.980
need to be a lot more responsible about how&nbsp;
much they're producing in the first place.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:35.640
SIERRA: Right. Like you don't want it&nbsp;
to go out on the open market, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:38.940
for half price, fair enough,&nbsp;
then don't produce as much.

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:41.100
SEGRAN: Exactly. And so this is going to put the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:44.400
onus on them to be much better&nbsp;
at tracking supply and demand.

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:47.880
SIERRA: Aside from a few ambitious policy makers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:52.980
there are also scientists and designers who are&nbsp;
trying to find creative solutions to the problem.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:21:00.420
One of these solutions is algae. Marine microalgae&nbsp;
can be used to make clothes that are biodegradable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:07.320
and far less energy and water intensive than other&nbsp;
fabrics. Or how about leather made from mushrooms?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:13.860
No cows, no methane, no problem. And there’s&nbsp;
a lot more to come. But, for the time being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:19.620
it can feel like all the existing options are&nbsp;
harmful. So what can we do in the meantime?

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:25.440
GREER: So, you could ask your&nbsp;
favorite companies, rhetorically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:29.460
on Twitter or send them an e-mail,&nbsp;
how transparent is your supply chain?&nbsp;

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:34.260
Do you evaluate your suppliers for their&nbsp;
environmental performance before you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:39.420
qualify them to make your stuff? What are&nbsp;
the consequences of egregious environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:44.880
behavior? Have you benchmarked your suppliers&nbsp;
for who has the lowest carbon footprint? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:52.800
make some very tangible requests of them, to try&nbsp;
to pressure them to up their game and be real.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:58.440
VALLETTA: Really take stock in the&nbsp;
things that could maybe be altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:03.840
and be made more modern for today. Simply&nbsp;
like changing a hem length. Guys, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:06.840
cut off those pants and make them into shorts.

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:11.120
SIERRA: Don't encourage them&nbsp;
to make jean shorts. No.

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:17.220
VALLETTA: But simple things. Like if you have&nbsp;
a favorite pair of jeans, they might be kinda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:24.420
cool with a patch. You know, dying a blouse,&nbsp;
with some natural dye. Just rethinking what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:29.820
you have and making it last longer. You know,&nbsp;
play around with your wardrobe and you can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:35.760
subtle changes. Have swaps with your friends.&nbsp;
Also, if you're going to clean out your closet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:41.040
don't throw anything in the trash,&nbsp;
find a recycling bin for clothing.

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:48.120
SIERRA: So I’m still looking around my&nbsp;
closet, and I’m still seeing things I love.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:56.880
But, you know, I’m having a hard time now&nbsp;
un-seeing the global cost of each piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:04.260
the path each piece took to get to me. Whether&nbsp;
that's wastewater, whether that's carbon in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:11.400
air, whether that’s dried up rivers. It all is&nbsp;
here hanging in front of me too. And you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:18.780
Amber’s right, I can’t undo what I’ve already&nbsp;
bought, they’re now mine, they are part of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:24.000
footprint, as well as part of my closet.&nbsp;
But I guess the best possible thing I can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:30.900
is get the best use out of the things that I&nbsp;
have here, with me now. Ok so there's a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:35.280
of stuff in here that I guess I can update&nbsp;
a bit, but there's also a lot in here that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:40.440
I can give to my friends. Which is the best&nbsp;
part, so let's start making some piles for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:46.380
my friends. Ok well first of all obviously this&nbsp;
striped shirt would look really good on Frida...

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:56.040
SEGRAN: I think we're at the very beginning,&nbsp;
and this is what scares me because there's so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.040 --> 00:24:04.440
much that we know now about how quickly the Earth&nbsp;
is going to be impacted by climate change. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:08.280
we really need to speed this whole process&nbsp;
up if we're really going to effect change.

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:17.880
SIERRA: Okay, so without a blockbuster statistic,&nbsp;
how do you convey the urgency of this problem?

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:23.220
GREER: So, people need to learn to live&nbsp;
without that blockbuster statistic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:29.280
but here's what for sure is true. The tremendous&nbsp;
amount of clothing that they buy is having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:34.920
tremendous impact. They can be assured that if&nbsp;
they buy less, they will have less impact and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:39.120
if the clothes they buy come from companies&nbsp;
that are more responsible, it will be even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:45.180
better. So they don't need specific numbers&nbsp;
to know that, basically, the magnitude of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:50.040
this problem is very large, very significant&nbsp;
and there's something they can do about it.

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:59.520
SIERRA: Look, there’s no industry more sensitive&nbsp;
to consumer preferences than fashion. And if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:04.320
can truly make waste uncool, if we&nbsp;
can stigmatize needless production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:09.240
then the industry, and the regulators who&nbsp;
have the power to govern it, can change.

00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:12.180
So… buy accordingly.

